r of  nightmares to the monolith about which  it flying it's gigantic scaly arms the  while it bow its hideous head and gave  vent to certain measured sounds I think  I went mad then my frantic is sent off  the slope wouldn't live and of my  delirious journey back to the stranded  boat I remember this  I believe our sang a great deal and  laughed oddly when I was unable to sing  I have indistinct recollections great  storm  sometime after I reach the boat at any  rate I know that I heard peals of  thunder and other tones which nature  utters only in her wildest moons when I  came out of the shadows I was in a San  Francisco Hospital brought thither by  the captain of the American ship which  had picked up my boat in mid-ocean in my  delirium I had said much but found that  my words me given scant attention of any  land upheaval in the Pacific my rescuers  knew nothing nor did i demon necessary  to insist upon a thing which I knew they  could not believe once I sought out a  celebrated ethnologist an amused him  with peculiar questions regarding the  ancient Philistine legend of Dagon the  fish gone but soon perceiving that he  was hopelessly conventional and I did  not press my inquiries  it is at night especially when the moon  is give us and waiting that I see the  thing I tried morphine but the drug has  given only transient Circe's and this  drawn me into its clutches is a hopeless  slave so now I am to end it all having  written a full account for the  information or the contemptuous  amusement of my fellow man often I ask  myself if it could not all have been a  pure phantasm a mere freak of Fever As I  Lay son stricken and raving in the open  boat after my escape from the German man  of war this I asked myself but ever does  there come before me a hideously vivid  vision in reply I cannot think of the  deep sea without shuddering of the  nameless things that made this very  moment to be crawling and floundering on  its slimy bed worshiping their ancient  ston